WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.633
The decoder.

2
00:00:01.933 --> 00:00:03.933
GPT, Claude, Llama, Mistral.

3
00:00:03.933 --> 00:00:07.933
One small change to the encoder makes them all possible.

4
00:00:09.133 --> 00:00:14.900
A decoder is the same attention and feed-forward stack, with one change that changes everything.

5
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:16.267
A causal mask.

6
00:00:16.567 --> 00:00:19.339
Each token may only look at tokens before it.

7
00:00:19.339 --> 00:00:20.633
The future is hidden.

8
00:00:21.833 --> 00:00:23.400
Here is the mask as a grid.

9
00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:25.600
The first token sees only itself.

10
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:29.733
The third token sees itself and the two before it, and nothing after.

11
00:00:30.033 --> 00:00:31.658
The result is a triangle.

12
00:00:31.658 --> 00:00:33.867
Each row depends only on the past.

13
00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:36.433
Put them side by side.

14
00:00:36.733 --> 00:00:39.877
The encoder sees everything in one parallel pass.

15
00:00:39.877 --> 00:00:41.867
The decoder sees only the past.

16
00:00:42.167 --> 00:00:45.900
One outputs vectors, the other a next token distribution.

17
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.583
So why can't BERT chat?

18
00:00:47.583 --> 00:00:52.633
Bidirectional attention leaks the future, so there's no honest next word to predict.

19
00:00:53.833 --> 00:00:57.167
At inference, the decoder generates left to right in a loop.

20
00:00:57.467 --> 00:00:59.033
Read the tokens so far.

21
00:00:59.333 --> 00:01:01.533
Predict a distribution for the next one.

22
00:01:01.833 --> 00:01:02.733
Pick a token.

23
00:01:03.033 --> 00:01:05.163
Append it, and go around again.

24
00:01:05.163 --> 00:01:09.833
Past computations never change, so a KV cache keeps generation fast.

25
00:01:11.033 --> 00:01:12.600
Here's a decoder in code.

26
00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.833
We load GPT two, a causal language model.

27
00:01:16.133 --> 00:01:19.942
We feed it a prompt and look at the logits for the last position only.

28
00:01:19.942 --> 00:01:21.900
That's the score for the next token.

29
00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.233
We softmax them and keep the top five candidates.

30
00:01:26.433 --> 00:01:27.700
Here's where you feel it.

31
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.167
Your endpoint streams the RAG answer token by token.

32
00:01:31.467 --> 00:01:35.500
That works because each new token depends only on what's already written.

33
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:40.148
And thanks to the KV cache, each extra token costs one forward step.

34
00:01:40.148 --> 00:01:42.067
An encoder could never stream.

35
00:01:43.267 --> 00:01:44.073
Let's recap.

36
00:01:44.073 --> 00:01:47.233
A decoder is the same stack plus a causal mask.

37
00:01:47.533 --> 00:01:48.967
It sees only the past.

38
00:01:49.267 --> 00:01:51.600
It outputs a next-token distribution.

39
00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:54.440
And every chat model is decoder-only.

40
00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:56.500
Next up: multi-head attention.
